gametitle=Champions: Return to Arms (SLUS-20973) v2.00

[Net test: cutscene audio sync off]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Stops cutscenes from nudging the speaking character's animation clock toward the local audio stream position (cut_advance, 0x199448). Cosmetic effect: lip movement may drift from the voice line. Every player in a game must use the same setting.
patch=1,EE,00199448,word,10000060

[Net test: prediction copy starts without pending damage]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Each machine predicts its own player on a throwaway copy. The copy is cloned with any damage still waiting on the real player, can die from it during prediction, and its death rebuilds the alive-player list on that one machine (FINDINGS G11-G18). This makes the clone start with no pending damage (Player_clone, 0x11EA8C), so the copy never dies ahead of the real player. Real deaths are untouched. Per machine; does not need to match between players. Replaces an earlier test patch at 0x1B3504, withdrawn because it changed real deaths (G21).
// was: E60000C8 (swc1 f0,0xc8(s0))  now: sw zero,0xc8(s0)
patch=1,EE,0011EA8C,word,AE0000C8

[Net test: equalise at once (LAG-1)]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V16: delay after a stall peaks at 11 frames instead of 34 and clears in 10 s instead of 35); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=net_main, 0x133574: skip sampling input whenever this machine's input is 5 or more frames further ahead of its simulation than the smallest such lead reported by the others, instead of only after 31 calls in a row. Keeps the extra input delay after an outage, a frozen peer or a slow peer to about 4 frames above the others. During a stall your own character stops too. Per machine. docs/issues/lag.md.
// was: 2862001F (slti v0,v1,31)
patch=1,EE,00133574,word,28620001

[Net test: trim leaves one spare frame (LAG-2)]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=net_main, 0x1335E4: the periodic input skip needs 4 ready frames instead of 3. Fewer one-frame world freezes after a lost packet, about 10 ms more input delay. Per machine. docs/issues/lag.md.
// was: 28420003 (slti v0,v0,3)
patch=1,EE,001335E4,word,28420004

[Net test: 4-player send cycle 6 -> 4 (LAG-3)]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Send-cycle table, 0x4027F4: with three remote peers each one is sent a packet every 4th call instead of every 6th. Do not use a value below 4: game messages need one call without a send. Per machine. docs/issues/lag.md.
// was: 00000006
patch=1,EE,004027F4,word,00000004

[Net test: 3-player send cycle 4 -> 3 (LAG-4)]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Send-cycle table, 0x4027F0: with two remote peers each one is sent a packet every 3rd call instead of every 4th. Do not use a value below 3. Per machine. docs/issues/lag.md.
// was: 00000004
patch=1,EE,004027F0,word,00000003

[Net test: peer silence time-out 900 -> 1440 calls]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two and three linked Play! emulator instances (a machine standing still for 1300 frames is kept and the pair stays in sync, one standing still for 1500 is dropped in the other's call 1442; with three machines the two others drop it in the same tick and stay in sync; docs/issues/online-quality.md 1.7); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=net_main, 0x133060: a peer from which no packet has been accepted is dropped after 1441 calls in a row (24 s at 60 calls a second) instead of 901 (15 s). Keeps a session through a longer emulator pause or host hang. Chosen against the HUD's own thresholds (lost-connection line after 1500 stalled calls; with more than two players a machine leaves after 2100): with two players the drop comes before the line, with three or four the drop is carried out 30-60 calls after it starts (measured: stall of 1458-1484 calls), so the line can show for a moment. When a peer really is gone the others wait 24 s instead of 15; the host's kick entry is not affected. Per machine: players need not match on it. docs/issues/online-quality.md.
// was: 28630385 (slti v1,v1,901)
patch=1,EE,00133060,word,286305A1

[Net test: kick request is answered for a peer already dropped]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on three and four linked Play! emulator instances (the host kicks one player: without the section every machine but the host never drops that player and all of them stand still for good, with it they drop it 30 frames after the host and play on in sync; docs/issues/online-quality.md 4.2); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=net_pumpReceive, 0x13C2F4: a machine asked to confirm a drop (packet 0xBD41) answers with its copy of that player's last frames even if it has already dropped the player itself. In the original it stays silent then, the asking machine never gets a drop frame, its game stops at that frame and the others stop behind it (FINDINGS L7). Three- and four-player games only. It is the answering machine that needs it, so every player should have it; mixing is harmless. docs/issues/online-quality.md.
// was: 1440002F (bnez v0,0x13c3b4)  now: nop
patch=1,EE,0013C2F4,word,00000000

[Net test: a peer being dropped still bounds the input lead]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on three and four linked Play! emulator instances (one machine stopped, or kicked: on the machines that go on the input lead at the drop is 13-18 frames instead of 33-38, and 4-15 ten seconds later instead of 14-29; docs/issues/online-quality.md 3.1); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=net_main, 0x1333B0: the loop that finds the smallest input lead and the slowest simulation frame among the other machines leaves a peer out once its drop frame is agreed (peer +0x44D8 above 0), not as soon as its drop is being negotiated (+0x44D6 not 0). In the original the 30-60 calls of the negotiation let every remaining machine sample input unchecked up to the 38-frame cap, which then drains at one frame a second. Three- and four-player games only; does its work together with LAG-1. Per machine. docs/issues/online-quality.md.
// was: 80E20006 (lb v0,6(a3)), 1440000F (bnez v0,0x1333f4)  now: lw v0,8(a3); bgtz v0,0x1333f4
patch=1,EE,001333B0,word,8CE20008
patch=1,EE,001333B4,word,1C40000F

[Net test: flush before a level load waits for the peers' tick]
author=reverse engineering test patch; this form confirmed on a native pair and on two linked Play! emulator instances from power-on (docs/issues/native-soak.md finding 1); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=net_flushBeforeLoad, 0x132DF8, 0x132DFC and 0x132E28: as before - at least 16 passes before a level load, then wait until every other machine has reported the simulation frame of the level change (peer +0x44C8) - but only when there are peers. The 16-pass test sits in the delay slot of the "no peers" branch, so the section as first written also made 16 passes of net_main with no peers at all: in the developer LAN start (boot argument --net) the main loop calls this function before net_gameInit, the passes ran on a netcode that was not set up, and both players' lockstep pads stayed stuck for the whole game (held word 0x500000, left stick -1.138). With the branch going straight to the function's exit the no-peers case makes no pass, as in the original. Per machine. docs/issues/online-quality.md 3.2.
// was: 18400011 (blez v0,0x132e40), 0000302D (move a2,zero), 8C82FFF0 (lw v0,-0x10(a0))  now: blez v0,0x132e70; slti a2,s0,16; lw v0,-8(a0)
patch=1,EE,00132DF8,word,1840001D
patch=1,EE,00132DFC,word,2A060010
patch=1,EE,00132E28,word,8C82FFF8

[Arena diagnostic: knight never summons]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Diagnostic only, changes the fight. The fourth knight of the Drundar arena never casts Summon Skeleton (Horsemen_run, 0x2726F4). If the arena still goes out of sync with this on every machine, the skeletons are not involved. Every player must use the same setting.
// was: 1462000D (bne v1,v0,+13)
patch=1,EE,002726F4,word,1000000D

[Arena diagnostic: pool reaper off]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Diagnostic only. The low-memory reaper never deletes expendable objects (runObjects, 0x14B9CC). If the object pool really runs low, new objects silently fail to appear. Tests FINDINGS D21. Every player must use the same setting.
// was: 2842012C (slti v0,v0,300)
patch=1,EE,0014B9CC,word,28420000

[Arena diagnostic: generic talk animation in cutscenes]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Diagnostic only. Cutscene speakers always use the generic talk loop instead of the per-line lip-sync animation, which also stops the audio-driven animation nudge (cut_execKey, 0x196AA0). Lips no longer follow the voice. Tests FINDINGS D8 and engine P6. Every player should use the same setting.
// was: 0062102A (slt v0,v1,v0)
patch=1,EE,00196AA0,word,00001021

[Arena diagnostic: never judge activity from the camera]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Diagnostic only. In a net game object activity is always judged from player positions, never from the cutscene camera (gameSetObjectVis, 0x1E4DD4). Scenery far from the players stops animating during cutscenes. Tests FINDINGS D6; also takes the camera out of the smoke and wave objects of D35 and the fire bombs of D29. Every player must use the same setting.
// was: 8043FCD0 (lb v1,-0x330(v0))
patch=1,EE,001E4DD4,word,00001821

[Net test: CloudGiant king conjure test uses a shared flag]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=The CloudGiant king decides whether to conjure a new bolt by testing a visual beam handle, which can differ between machines (CloudGiant run, 0x250930). This makes it test the simulation's own "a thrown bolt is in flight" flag instead. In normal play the two agree. FINDINGS D28. Every player must use the same setting.
// was: 8E420530 (lw v0,0x530(s2))
patch=1,EE,00250930,word,8E420508

[Diagnostic: fire bombs ignore the view test]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Diagnostic only, changes gameplay. Bomb throwers launch on their timer whether or not anything is near the view (FireBomb_run, 0x261FD0), so the launch no longer depends on the camera during cutscenes. More objects alive in levels that have them. FINDINGS D29. Every player must use the same setting.
// was: 0C079DF0 (jal isNearView)
patch=1,EE,00261FD0,word,24020001

[Net test: net launch clears a stuck memory-card latch]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=A save that ends in a memory-card error leaves a "card busy" latch set for the rest of the boot; a machine in that state skips the clean-up every other machine does when the party changes plane, and goes out of sync there. This makes the net launch code clear the latch (net launch, 0x1375DC). The byte it used to clear instead is never set by anything. FINDINGS B10, B11. Harmless when only some players use it.
// was: A0408F2A (sb zero,-0x70d6(v0))
patch=1,EE,001375DC,word,A0408F28

[Net test: save-point prompt ignores this machine's card screen]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=While one machine has a memory-card screen open (usually its own save dialog), another player's use of a save point is not registered on that machine, so the "active player" used by level scripts can differ for a moment. This removes the card test from the prompt (Savepoint prompt, 0x28DA74); the save point's own second test still stops a second dialog. The prompt text can show while your own card screen is open. FINDINGS B9. Each machine fixes its own copy; use on every machine.
// was: 54400069 (bnezl v0,0x28dc1c)
patch=1,EE,0028DA74,word,00000000

[Net test: ignore the debug file on the memory card]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=A debug.sht file on one player's memory card switches on developer cheats that reach the simulation, which breaks sync within minutes. This stops the game from ever setting that switch (config load, 0x217448). Only matters on a machine that has the file; removes the debug menu there. Sync-breakers document, section 7.
// was: A2220000 (sb v0,0(s1))
patch=1,EE,00217448,word,00000000

[Net test: use button works with the spell selector open]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed to remove the desync on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V10, V11); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=While you hold the D-pad (quick-spell selector), your own machine skips the "use" action for your character but the other machines do not. A use press that was already on its way when the D-pad went down then opens a chest or picks up an item on the other machines only. This removes the skip (Player_run, 0x1A5C0C), so every machine does the same. In a net game your new use presses are still filtered out while the selector is open. Offline, use now also works with the selector open. FINDINGS K1. Each machine fixes its own player; a mix is safe.
// was: 54600006 (bnezl v1,0x1a5c28)
patch=1,EE,001A5C0C,word,00000000

[Net test: potions always leave the bag directly]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed to remove the desync on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V10, V11); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=If your inventory screen is open when a potion press is simulated, your machine sends a "drop" message instead of removing the potion, while the other machines remove it and then obey the message too, so the potion counts differ from then on. This makes the potion always leave the bag directly (Player_usePotion, health branch 0x1B9FCC and mana branch 0x1BA3AC). Your open inventory screen may show the old count until reopened. FINDINGS K2. Each machine fixes its own player; a mix is safe.
// was: 8043B69D (lb v1,-0x4963(v0)) at both addresses
patch=1,EE,001B9FCC,word,00001821
patch=1,EE,001BA3AC,word,00001821

[Net test: stats page does not rewrite stored weapon damage]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V42 (the record difference; a hit that reads it was not run)); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=For a character with two weapons, the stats page recomputes damage for the off-hand weapon and leaves those numbers in the character record on that machine only until the next recompute. This skips that second call (stats page, 0x1D2D30). The second damage range printed on the stats page then repeats the first. FINDINGS K3. Each machine for itself; a mix is safe.
// was: 0C06DC56 (jal Player_recalcDamage)
patch=1,EE,001D2D30,word,00000000

[Arena diagnostic: no prediction copy]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Diagnostic only. This machine never makes the prediction copy of its own player (net_frameLoop, 0x13B21C), so your character is drawn from the real object and your own input looks late. Must be on before the first level of a session. If the arena still goes out of sync with this on, the prediction copy is not involved. FINDINGS E6. Touches nothing shared; for a clean test use it on every machine.
// was: 106000DF (beqz v1,+0xdf)
patch=1,EE,0013B21C,word,100000DF

[Arena diagnostic: cutscenes end in the tick they start]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Diagnostic only. Every in-level cutscene behaves as if skipped at once (cut_runFrame, 0x199DF8): no cutscene is seen and voice lines are cut. If the arena still goes out of sync with this on, the opening cutscene is not needed for it. FINDINGS E13. Every player must use the same setting; a mix desyncs at the first cutscene.
// was: 10400004 (beqz v0,+4)
patch=1,EE,00199DF8,word,00000000

[Arena diagnostic: no autosave on entering a level]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=Diagnostic only. No checkpoint autosave is armed after a level load on this machine (save_armAutosave, 0x217F58). After a party wipe the reload then uses an older snapshot. Tests FINDINGS E8. For a clean test use it on every machine.
// was: 24030001 (addiu v1,zero,1)
patch=1,EE,00217F58,word,24030000

[Net test: closing a notice box does not run the pending script]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V40); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=Closing one of the small per-player notice boxes runs the level's pending "post action" script from drawing code on that machine only; the full-screen help box already skips this in a net game. This removes the call (help task, 0x1DD1F0). Offline, a post action meant to follow a notice waits for the next cutscene, dialog or level load. FINDINGS E9. Each machine fixes itself; use on every machine.
// was: 0C07B6B0 (jal script_runPostAction)
patch=1,EE,001DD1F0,word,00000000

[Net test: menu lockout never drops player damage]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V39); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=When your character dies with the inventory or shop open, closing that menu makes your machine alone ignore all damage to players for the rest of that tick. This makes the damage code read the lockout as off (Player_takeDamage, 0x1B53D4). Offline, players can be hurt in the second after a menu closes. FINDINGS D26, U3. Each machine for itself; a mix is safe.
// was: 8C43B4FC (lw v1,-0x4b04(v0))
patch=1,EE,001B53D4,word,00001821

[Net test: menu lockout never blocks weapon swap and use]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V39); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=Companion to the patch above: the same lockout also made one machine skip weapon swap and "use" for players that run later in that tick (Player_run, 0x1A5BA8). Offline, the press that closed a menu can also swap weapons or interact. FINDINGS D26, U3. Each machine for itself; a mix is safe.
// was: 8E02B4FC (lw v0,-0x4b04(s0))
patch=1,EE,001A5BA8,word,00001021

[Net test: option commit leaves the button maps alone]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V35); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=For online test sessions only. Leaving the pause menu's option list after changing any option copies this machine's own saved controller layouts over every player's button map, so a player with remapped buttons is then read wrongly on that machine. This jumps over that copy (config_commitOptions, 0x1E6A60). Side effect in every mode: a change made in the game's controls screen no longer takes effect when you leave the options. FINDINGS U1. Each machine for itself; a mix is safe.
// was: 3C060042 (lui a2,0x42); now j 0x1e6b10
patch=1,EE,001E6A60,word,08079AC4

[Net test: mission menu ignores the chooser's pause menu]
author=reverse engineering test patch, untested on a running game
description=While the player choosing a mission has the pause menu open, that machine alone ignores the chooser's button presses in the mission menu. This makes it read them like every other machine (missionMenu_run, 0x231A64). Presses made in the pause menu then also reach the mission menu underneath, on every machine alike. FINDINGS U2. A mix is safe.
// was: 10400006 (beqz v0,+6)
patch=1,EE,00231A64,word,10000006

[Net test: jump-gate menu ignores the chooser's pause menu]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V41); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=The same as the mission-menu patch, for the jump-gate destination menu (jumpgate_menuRun, 0x27DEF8). FINDINGS U2. A mix is safe.
// was: 50400006 (beqzl v0,+6)
patch=1,EE,0027DEF8,word,10000006

[Net test: skeleton queries rebuild inside the tick]
author=reverse engineering test patch, executed in the function-level interpreter, untested on a running game
description=The game caches each skeleton's pose and lets drawing fill the cache, so game logic that asks for a bone position can get a pose from before the last animation change on a machine that drew the character and a fresh one on a machine that did not. With this, any such question asked during a simulation step throws the cached pose away and rebuilds it (anim_getJointMatrix, 0x16F73C, with a 12-word routine written over the unused function at 0x257AB4). Drawing and offline play are unchanged. Costs a pose build per question; expect a slow frame during large particle bursts. It also changes a few debris and effect positions for everyone, so EVERY player must use the same setting. No route from this cache to the arena desync was found; if the arena stays in sync only with this on, there is one. FINDINGS D34, D39, X5; docs/issues/pose-cache.md section 8.
// was: 14400004 27BDFFB0 24020001 FFB00030 0080802D FFB10038 0000882D 14A2000F FFBF0040 8E020024 8E0500E8 0C05E554 8C44000C (the branch, then the first 12 words of the unused function, in address order)
patch=1,EE,0016F73C,word,08095EAD
patch=1,EE,00257AB4,word,10400007
patch=1,EE,00257AB8,word,3C030040
patch=1,EE,00257ABC,word,8C6339B8
patch=1,EE,00257AC0,word,3C010002
patch=1,EE,00257AC4,word,14610005
patch=1,EE,00257AC8,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,00257ACC,word,AC400610
patch=1,EE,00257AD0,word,AE000000
patch=1,EE,00257AD4,word,0805BDD1
patch=1,EE,00257AD8,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,00257ADC,word,0805BDD4
patch=1,EE,00257AE0,word,00000000

[Net test: script return keeps the saved stack pointer]
author=reverse engineering test patch, executed in the function-level interpreter, untested on a running game
description=Some level scripts in the hub and three other levels (for example the store) call a built-in command with fewer arguments than they declare. Each time, the script machine's saved stack pointer ends 4 too high, and from the second time the amount is read from whatever memory follows the script file. This makes a script return store the stack pointer the run started with (script interpreter RETN, 0x13FC24), so nothing drifts. All 1,828 script entry points behave the same with it in the interpreter. FINDINGS SM1. Changes no shared state by itself; each machine that uses it is protected.
// was: AEF60018 (sw s6,0x18(s7))
patch=1,EE,0013FC24,word,AEEC0018

[Net test: predicted spin cast leaves the shared animation alone]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V19: 6 of 6 same-class pairs diverged without it, 0 of 6 with it); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=When a character casts the spin skill, the game writes a timing value into an animation that every character of that class shares - even when the cast is only your own machine's early prediction of your character. Two players of the same class who both spin then go out of sync. With this, a predicted cast skips those two writes; the real cast still makes them (SpellCyclone constructor, 0x2D1978, with a 10-word routine in the unused function at 0x257AE4, after the words the pose-cache section uses). FINDINGS SB1. Changes nothing shared by itself; each machine that uses it is protected, so use it on every machine.
// was: A0620018 E4630014 8E0300E8 0040882D 2402FFCF 8E040024 00621824 AE0300E8 0060282D 0C05E554 8C84000C 02228826 (the two stores, then ten words of the unused function, in address order)
patch=1,EE,002D1978,word,08095EB9
patch=1,EE,002D197C,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,00257AE4,word,8E01000C
patch=1,EE,00257AE8,word,8C21000C
patch=1,EE,00257AEC,word,00010C42
patch=1,EE,00257AF0,word,30210001
patch=1,EE,00257AF4,word,14200003
patch=1,EE,00257AF8,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,00257AFC,word,A0620018
patch=1,EE,00257B00,word,E4630014
patch=1,EE,00257B04,word,080B4660
patch=1,EE,00257B08,word,00000000

[Net test: creatures ignore being touched by the prediction copy]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V22: the pet pair that diverged stays in step with it); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=When a player touches a creature, the creature is flagged and reacts on its next step. The game also does this when the thing touching is your own machine's prediction copy of your character, which runs a few steps ahead and exists on your machine only - so your pet (always next to you), or a monster you bump into, reacts earlier on your machine than on anyone else's. On the live rig this put a summoned pet out of sync in the arena. With this, a creature is flagged only by a real player (Creature touch handler, rewritten in place, 0x11A41C-0x11A440). FINDINGS G25, V18. Changes nothing shared by itself; each machine that uses it is protected, so use it on every machine.
// was: 30420040 10400006 00000000 DC8200A8 00031B3C 00431025 FC8200A8 03E00008 00000000
patch=1,EE,0011A41C,word,00021182
patch=1,EE,0011A420,word,30420801
patch=1,EE,0011A424,word,2442FFFF
patch=1,EE,0011A428,word,14400005
patch=1,EE,0011A430,word,DC8200A8
patch=1,EE,0011A434,word,00031B3C
patch=1,EE,0011A438,word,00431025
patch=1,EE,0011A43C,word,FC8200A8
patch=1,EE,0011A440,word,03E00008

[Net test: item records start from zero, not from stack leftovers]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V30: all twelve real-character arena pairs agree with it, where two still differed silently without it); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=When the game creates an item it starts from a default record of 72 bytes of which it sets only 26; the other 46 are whatever was left on the stack, and that differs between machines (for instance according to whether a skeleton pose was cached or rebuilt just before). Dropped loot then carries different unused bytes on different machines. This rewrites the default-record set-up in place so the whole record is cleared first (item descriptor parser, 0x1069FC-0x106A48). Fields a descriptor does not set become 0 instead of leftovers. FINDINGS I1, V23, V30. Every player should use the same setting.
// was: 24050020 24040003 00C51021 24A50008 2403FFFF 2484FFFF 00000000 0481FFFA A4430004 A0C30021 24040010 A0C30020 24050003 00C41021 24840002 0040182D 24A5FFFF A4400000 04A1FFFA A4600008
patch=1,EE,001069FC,word,FCC00000
patch=1,EE,00106A00,word,FCC00008
patch=1,EE,00106A04,word,FCC00010
patch=1,EE,00106A08,word,FCC00018
patch=1,EE,00106A0C,word,FCC00020
patch=1,EE,00106A10,word,FCC00028
patch=1,EE,00106A14,word,FCC00030
patch=1,EE,00106A18,word,FCC00038
patch=1,EE,00106A1C,word,FCC00040
patch=1,EE,00106A20,word,2403FFFF
patch=1,EE,00106A24,word,A4C30024
patch=1,EE,00106A28,word,A4C3002C
patch=1,EE,00106A2C,word,A4C30034
patch=1,EE,00106A30,word,A4C3003C
patch=1,EE,00106A34,word,A0C30021
patch=1,EE,00106A38,word,A0C30020
