[Net test: no touch handler runs for a prediction copy that walks into something]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V61: a small spider killed on one machine only stays alive on both with it; two arena pairs equal tick for tick with and without it); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=Every object has a "something touched me" routine, and 33 of the 38 versions of it never check whether the thing touching is your own machine's prediction copy of your character, which exists on your machine only; 30 of those change the shared game (a small spider dies when a player steps on it, containers and props are shoved, creatures are flagged). One place in the game can hand them the copy: the creature walk routine, which a player's copy runs while the player is under scripted control, for instance frozen by a cutscene. With this, that one call is skipped when the mover is a prediction copy (call at 0x114220 routed through eight words in unused code at 0x257B30-0x257B4C); real players and monsters reach the routines exactly as before. It makes the older section "creatures ignore being touched by the prediction copy" redundant, and the two can be on together. FINDINGS G25, G26, V61; docs/issues/touch-handlers.md. Changes nothing shared by itself; each machine that uses it is protected, so use it on every machine.
// was: 0060F809 26080114 6A030017 6E030010 8E070018 B0A30007 B4A30000 ACA70008 3C013F80
patch=1,EE,00114220,word,0C095ECC
patch=1,EE,00257B30,word,8CA1000C
patch=1,EE,00257B34,word,00010B80
patch=1,EE,00257B38,word,04200003
patch=1,EE,00257B3C,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,00257B40,word,00600008
patch=1,EE,00257B44,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,00257B48,word,03E00008
patch=1,EE,00257B4C,word,00000000

[Net test: save point is used on every machine whatever this machine's card screen does]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on native pairs and in a native soak of the set with this section (26 games of two, three and four machines, 1,779,709 ticks, no deciding difference: docs/issues/native-soak.md, "Second part"), and on two linked Play! emulator instances (with the other copy's card screen up the save point's state differs for good without it and not at all with it, 3,854 frames; a game of 13,123 lockstep frames with it, every sync hash equal: docs/issues/mac-app-start.md, "The three proposed sections on the emulator"); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=When a player presses use at a save point, every machine runs the save point's own code inside the tick (Savepoint_interact, 0x28DC30) - but a machine whose own memory-card screen is up at that moment (its save dialog, the options being written after a change in the pause menu) skips all of it, so the save point's state (+0x84, its light, its active flag and, were there any, its action scripts) then differs from the other machines': the "saved" burst, the point's private random numbers and the 6 bytes it writes into the level's stored state follow on some machines and not on others. This swaps the two tests and narrows the card one: the menu-mutex test (shared state in a net game) still guards everything, the card test guards only the request to open this machine's save dialog. Nothing else changes; a machine that was not busy does exactly what it did. Offline the only change is that the point lights up when used while a card screen is open. FINDINGS B9. Each machine fixes its own copy; use on every machine.
// was: 0C085D46 (jal memcard_isBusy), 0C08BF0B (jal menuMutex_isHeld), 1440000A (bnez v0,0x28dc88), 24100001 (addiu s0,zero,1), 0C085D6D (jal memcard_requestSave)  now: jal menuMutex_isHeld; jal memcard_isBusy; bnez v0,0x28dc68; jal memcard_requestSave; addiu s0,zero,1
patch=1,EE,0028DC44,word,0C08BF0B
patch=1,EE,0028DC54,word,0C085D46
patch=1,EE,0028DC5C,word,14400002
patch=1,EE,0028DC64,word,0C085D6D
patch=1,EE,0028DC68,word,24100001

[Net test: reading a quest variable never adds it to the table]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed to remove the difference on a native pair and in a native soak of the set with this section (26 games, 1,779,709 ticks, no deciding difference: docs/issues/native-soak.md, "Second part"); on two linked Play! emulator instances it is at work (the names only read are not added) in a game of 13,123 lockstep frames, every sync hash equal, but the race itself was not produced there (docs/issues/mac-app-start.md, "The three proposed sections on the emulator"); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=Reading a quest variable that does not exist yet (script getv, vars_get 0x1ED778) appends its name to the table with value 0. Level scripts are also run from drawing code - the prompt of a jump gate or a locked object for this machine's own player, the marker over a character's head, the map - so the machine that draws appends the name some ticks before the others, or alone, and the tables then differ in content or in order. This makes a read of a missing name return 0 and leave the table as it is; names are added only by a store (setv), which every machine does in the same tick. Every value read is what it was (a missing name read 0 before, too); the table and the save file hold only variables that were ever set. docs/script.md P1. Per machine; a mix is as safe as the game is without it.
// was: 0C0EE1A3 (jal libc_strcpy)  now: b 0x1ed844 (v0 is 0 on both ways in)
patch=1,EE,001ED828,word,10000006

[Net test: a jump gate is found only inside the tick]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed to remove the difference on a native pair and in a native soak of the set with this section (26 games, 1,779,709 ticks, no deciding difference: docs/issues/native-soak.md, "Second part"), and on two linked Play! emulator instances (the hub's gate is found in the same lockstep frame on both copies with it and one frame apart without, two runs each; a game of 13,123 lockstep frames with it, every sync hash equal: docs/issues/mac-app-start.md, "The three proposed sections on the emulator"); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=The prompt of a jump gate (JumpGate_interactPrompt, 0x27D7A0) marks a gate nobody has found yet as found (+0x4D), adds the level to the list of jump-gate destinations (jumpgate_addCurrentLevel) and shows the "activated" message. The prompt runs inside the tick for every player, but also from drawing for this machine's own player, where the tests in front of it (menu open, camera flag) use this machine's current state and not the lockstep one: a player who closes the inventory beside an unfound gate has the gate found and the destination added on the owner's machine as many ticks early as its input runs ahead (19 ticks in the directed game), and for good if the player dies or the level changes before the tick gets there. This makes the prompt mark the gate only when it was called from the tick: Player_interact keeps its display-only argument in register s7 across the call (0x1B2650; the prompt has no other caller but the reflector's forwarding prompt, which leaves s7 alone), and the test "already found" becomes "already found, or called for display". The prompt's text and result at draw time are those of a found gate. Per machine; a mix is as safe as the game is without it.
// was: 14400010 (bnez v0,0x27d864), 3C02008E (lui v0,0x8e)  now: or v0,v0,s7; bnez v0,0x27d860 (which loads the same v0; the addiu v0,zero,1 behind it is now the delay slot and is overwritten there)
patch=1,EE,0027D820,word,00571025
patch=1,EE,0027D824,word,1440000E

[Net test: the save at a level's entry is taken in the same tick on every machine]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed to remove the desync on native pairs and on a four-machine game, and in a native soak of the set with it (26 games of two, three and four machines, 1,326,739 ticks, no difference: docs/issues/native-soak.md, third part, "A late save"); on two linked Play! emulator instances (docs/issues/mac-app-start.md, "The late-save and the strafe section on the emulator"): with one copy's inventory open when a level is entered both copies fill their save buffers in the entry tick and a party wipe loads the same records again, where without it that copy saved 254 ticks late and loaded other hit points for both players; untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=When a level has been entered every machine fills its save buffer with every player's record, the quest variables and the level states: the restart point a party that is wiped out is sent back to. The run task that does it (save_daemonRun, 0x2188A8) waits while this machine's own memory-card screen is up (the options being written after a change in the pause menu, a save dialog), while this machine's full-screen menu flag is set (inventory, shop, statistics) and while this machine's camera is not in follow mode (inventory or shop again - or a cutscene). A player who has the inventory open when somebody takes the party through a door therefore gets a restart point from the tick the menu closes, not from the level's first tick; when the whole party dies before the next common save, that machine restores other hit points, mana, bags and potions for everyone than the others do, and the game is out of step from the reload on (seen: a four-machine game, the game's own out-of-sync flag three ticks after the reload). With this the card test and the menu test are gone and the camera test is the shared cutscene flag (0x44FCD0) instead: the save waits for the end of a cutscene as before, on every machine alike, and for nothing that is one machine's own. Offline nothing changes: those screens pause the game there, and the pause test stays. The save only fills the buffer; a card write in progress is not touched (a level change waits for it). mainloop.md P3, audio-save.md "Checkpoint autosave". Each machine fixes its own save; use on every machine.
// was: 14A40027 (bne a1,a0,0x2189d4: this machine's card state is not idle), 14400016 (bnez v0,0x2189d8: this machine's full-screen menu flag), 3C020050 8C428450 8C420034 38420001 (lui v0,0x50; lw v0,-0x7bb0(v0); lw v0,0x34(v0); xori v0,v0,1: this machine's camera is not in mode 1)  now: nop; nop; lui v0,0x45; lb v0,-0x330(v0); nop; nop (the shared cutscene flag 0x44FCD0)
patch=1,EE,00218934,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,0021897C,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,00218980,word,3C020045
patch=1,EE,00218984,word,8042FCD0
patch=1,EE,00218988,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,0021898C,word,00000000

[Net test: strafe mode - a player can run one way and face another]
author=first-person controls work, 2026-10-13; executed in the function-level interpreter (tools/recomp/app/fp_section_test.py); confirmed on two, three and four native copies in one lockstep game and on a native copy with an emulator-based copy (docs/issues/native-first-person-controls.md part 3); in a native soak with the mode in use on every machine (31 games, 1,842,998 ticks: docs/issues/native-soak.md, third part); on two linked Play! emulator instances (docs/issues/mac-app-start.md, "The late-save and the strafe section on the emulator"): the mode set and cleared through the section's own sender on one copy, the byte changing in the same lockstep tick on both, position, facing and mode byte equal after every one of 937 ticks while that player ran sideways and backwards, a level change clearing it on both; untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=The game runs a character along its facing and turns the facing to the stick: nobody can run sideways or backwards while facing ahead, which a view from the character's eyes needs. This gives each player a mode byte (player object +0x47E, a byte only the unreachable jump state used). While the byte is 0 - for everyone who never asks for the mode - every tick is exactly as without this section. While it is not 0, in the ordinary moving state the character turns (40 % of the angle a tick, the game's own rate) to where its player's camera looks - the heading a pushed-up stick asks for, 0x3E3D minus the yaw word that already travels with every controller row - and runs along the stick's own direction instead of along its facing, at the same speed. The mode is set by game message 4, sub-type 2 {2, slot, mode}; message 4 is the game's own "null" channel, carried and executed in lockstep with an empty handler (its one sender in the game, sub-type 1 when a shop closes, is still ignored). Every machine sets the byte in the same tick. Player_ctor now clears the byte, so every new character (level start, respawn) starts with the mode off on every machine. Parts: the hook in Player_move (0x1B4210); its code in the unreferenced function 0x2128C8; a sender in the unreferenced function 0x215C98 (called with the mode in a0; only a build that offers the mode calls it); the handler in the unreferenced function 0x215D28, reached by a jump from the empty handler 0x1E01B0; and the two byte stores of the constructor. Every player must have this section or none.
// was: 24020005 12A2007C (Player_move 0x1b4210), A2A0047C A2A0047D (Player_ctor 0x1a22fc), 03E00008 00000000 (the empty handler of game message 4, 0x1e01b0), 27BDFFD0 3C020087 FFBF0020 FFB00010 24506C00 FFB10018 24110002 00000000 8E040000 10800004 2631FFFF 0C0D6B12 00000000 AE000000 0621FFF9 26100004 3C020087 24030002 24426C10 24420030 2463FFFF AC40FFFC AC400000 00000000 00000000 0461FFFA 244201C4 3C070087 3C040087 3C090087 24847160 24E77200 252971C0 240500A0 0000302D 0000402D 240A0040 0000582D 0C0D59BC AFA00000 DFB00010 DFB10018 DFBF0020 03E00008 27BD0030 (the unreferenced function 0x2128c8, all of its 45 words), 27BDFFF0 FFB00000 FFBF0008 0C085A18 0080802D 0040282D 14A00005 00000000 10000018 DFB00000 10000015 0000102D 0600000F 0000202D 00A0182D 80620000 (the first 16 of the 36 words of the unreferenced function 0x215c98), 27BDFFF0 3C020087 FFB00000 0080802D FFBF0008 0C05CDDF 244423E8 0040302D 50C00013 DFB00000 0600000C 0000282D 00C0202D 80830000 24840050 1060000B 0000102D (the first 17 of the 31 words of the unreferenced function 0x215d28)  now: the hook, the constructor's two stores, the jump to the handler, the move code, the sender, the handler - as noted block by block below
// Player_move 0x1b4210: j 0x2128c8; addiu v0,zero,0x5
patch=1,EE,001B4210,word,08084A32
patch=1,EE,001B4214,word,24020005
// Player_ctor 0x1a22fc: sh zero,0x47c(s5); sb zero,0x47e(s5)
patch=1,EE,001A22FC,word,A6A0047C
patch=1,EE,001A2300,word,A2A0047E
// the empty handler of game message 4, 0x1e01b0: j 0x215d28; nop
patch=1,EE,001E01B0,word,0808574A
patch=1,EE,001E01B4,word,00000000
// the move code, in the unreferenced function 0x2128c8 (45 words of its 45)
patch=1,EE,002128C8,word,16A20003
